// REJECTS: Unexpected token 'host'. Valid alternatives: ['protocol', 'httpPath', 'hostname', 'port']
//
// `host:` on a Databricks datasource specification, which spells it `hostname`. Every other
// vendor in this batch that has a machine address calls it `host` -- MemSql, Trino, Oracle,
// Aurora, Redshift, and the Static spec that predates all of them -- and Databricks alone
// does not.
//
// These are separate token namespaces, not one shared vocabulary with per-vendor subsets:
// DatabricksLexerGrammar declares its own four tokens and imports none of the others. So a
// rewrite backed by a single field table keyed on name, which is what the near-identical
// shape of these specs invites, accepts `host` here and would have to be told, vendor by
// vendor, which spelling each one owns.
//
// The alternative list in the message is the entire Databricks grammar: four tokens, all four
// required by the walker even though the .g4 makes every one optional.
###Connection
RelationalDatabaseConnection fixture::neg::DatabricksHost
{
   store: fixture::neg::Db;
   type: Databricks;
   specification: Databricks
   {
      host: 'dbc-1234.cloud.databricks.com';
      port: '443';
